            "creation_time": "2026-10-26T17:00:18Z",
            "text": "Byte-compiling an expression with `break` in the right-hand side of a complex assignment can segfault R. I reproduced this on R 4.6.1; OSS-Fuzz also reproduced it with R-devel r90763. Evaluating the same expression without compilation returns normally.\n\nReproducible example:\n\n    e <- parse(text = \"1 - repeat x[1] <- break\")[[1L]]\n    eval(e)                     # numeric(0)\n    eval(compiler::compile(e))  # segfault\n\nThe compiled expression should return numeric(0), as the uncompiled expression does. Instead, R crashes with an invalid read in `Rf_protect()` called from `Rf_endcontext()`.\n\nAnalysis:\n\nIn `src/library/compiler/R/cmp.R`, `cmpComplexAssign()` emits `INCLNKSTK` before compiling the assignment value. Its non-tail-call context still allows an inlined `break` to compile as a direct `GOTO`, which skips the matching `DECLNKSTK`. The leftover link-stack offset is then read as loop-context storage by `ENDLOOPCNTXT`, causing the crash.\n\nProposed patch: attached as compiler-complex-assign-break.diff, based on trunk r90764. It compiles the assignment value in an argument context so `break` unwinds the loop context and the link-stack marker, and adds a regression test.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/409, where CI results can be seen.",
